---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import MediaPlaceholder from '../components/MediaPlaceholder.astro';
---
<Layout title="For Parents">
  <section class="section">
    <div class="container hero hero--split">
      <div>
        <h1>What are we trying to build for your child?</h1>
        <p>Aya &amp; Sura is a character world for Muslim children, but the goal is not to turn every story into a lesson or every activity into schoolwork. We want children to enjoy the characters first—and give parents stories, games and activities that can naturally support good conversations, useful skills and Muslim identity along the way.</p>
      </div>
      <MediaPlaceholder label="PARENT PAGE — product/story/activity laid out clearly; avoid generic stock parent photo" ratio="wide" variant={2} />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--surface">
    <div class="container">
      <h2>Different ages need different kinds of play.</h2>
      <p>Aya &amp; Sura is primarily building for children under 12, but we do not expect one product to fit every child from toddlerhood through the preteen years. Each book, deck, app experience and Adventure Club release should have its own recommended age and level of parent involvement.</p>
      <p>A younger child may meet Aya and Sura through read-aloud stories and parent-led play. A school-age child may use cards, puzzles and digital adventures more independently. As the world grows, we can create different experiences for different stages without turning Aya &amp; Sura into separate unrelated brands.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--soft">
    <div class="container">
      <h2>What kinds of things will Aya &amp; Sura explore?</h2>
      <div class="grid grid-4" style="margin-top:1.5rem;">
        <div class="card"><h3>Grow</h3><p class="muted">Patience, confidence, responsibility, emotions, honesty and trying again.</p></div>
        <div class="card"><h3>Connect</h3><p class="muted">Siblings, parents, friends, neighbors, sharing, empathy and community.</p></div>
        <div class="card"><h3>Wonder</h3><p class="muted">Nature, animals, food, creativity, questions, places and discovery.</p></div>
        <div class="card"><h3>Remember</h3><p class="muted">Muslim family life, gratitude to Allah, Quran familiarity, Ramadan, Eid, duas and other gentle faith connections where they fit naturally.</p></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--deep">
    <div class="container">
      <h2>Islam is part of the world—not pasted on top of it.</h2>
      <p>Aya &amp; Sura can talk about gratitude to Allah, Ramadan, Quran, generosity and other explicitly Islamic subjects. They can also have a disagreement with a friend, try a new recipe, visit another community or make a mistake at home. Muslim children deserve stories where faith can matter without every moment becoming a classroom.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--surface">
    <div class="container">
      <h2>What does "Muslim children's brand" mean here?</h2>
      <p>Aya &amp; Sura does not need to avoid Islam in order to serve a broad range of Muslim families. At the same time, not every product needs to teach detailed fiqh or theology. The core line focuses on broadly shared values, family life and familiar Muslim experiences. Topics involving detailed rulings or areas where communities differ require deliberate review rather than casual inclusion.</p>
      <p class="muted">Arabic and Quran learning can be part of Aya &amp; Sura, but they are not the only reason the characters exist.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <h2>Does my child need to be learning Arabic?</h2>
      <p>No. The flashcards and some optional activities can support Arabic or Quran vocabulary, but stories and Adventure Club content should still be useful to families who are not following an Arabic curriculum. When Arabic appears, the goal is to make it an approachable extension rather than a prerequisite.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--soft">
    <div class="container">
      <h2>Why have an app if you also make books and activities?</h2>
      <p>Because different formats are good at different things. A phone can play audio and carry content anywhere. A card can be held, sorted and played with. A coloring page invites a different kind of attention. A book works beautifully at bedtime. Aya &amp; Sura should use the format that fits the experience instead of forcing everything onto a screen.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--surface">
    <div class="container">
      <h2>You should not need to build the lesson yourself.</h2>
      <p>A major goal of the Adventure Club is to give parents something ready to use. Open the story. Print the activity. Ask one of the conversation questions if it feels useful. Skip the pieces your family does not need. The product should reduce planning, not create more of it.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--deep">
    <div class="container">
      <h2>Already used Aya &amp; Sura with your family?</h2>
      <p>We are actively reconnecting with families who purchased our earlier products. We want to know what your child actually used, what they ignored, whether the audio and games helped, and what you would like us to make next. Honest feedback is more valuable than praise.</p>
      <a class="btn btn-primary" style="background:#fff9f0;color:var(--brand-deep);" href="/feedback/">Share Your Experience</a>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--surface">
    <div class="container">
      <h2>FAQ</h2>
      <div class="faq">
        <details>
          <summary>How is Islam part of Aya &amp; Sura?</summary>
          <p>Aya &amp; Sura is created for Muslim families. Faith is part of the characters' everyday world—in how they give thanks to Allah, make choices, celebrate, care for people, learn and understand the world around them. Some stories will focus directly on Islamic topics. Others will begin with ordinary childhood experiences and let Muslim identity be a natural part of the story.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Is the Adventure Club physical?</summary>
          <p>The founding version is planned as a digital print-at-home experience. Physical options can be tested later based on what families actually want to receive.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Can I keep using my old flashcards and QR links?</summary>
          <p>Yes. Existing printed QR destinations are being treated as permanent product resources and will be preserved during the website transition.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Are the products only for homeschool families?</summary>
          <p>No. They can be used at home, alongside school, in homeschool settings or as supplemental activities.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Will there be toys?</summary>
          <p>Possibly later. Books, cards, digital content and lightweight printable/physical products are the near-term focus. More complex toys should follow proven character demand.</p>
        </details>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Layout>
